-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Missing Angular Coverage:** In scenes with missing views (e.g., corners), optimizing all SH bands from the start leads to incorrect diffuse colors. The staged introduction mitigates this [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Gradient Limiting:** Limiting gradient computation to the front-most $N$ Gaussians (as done in some other splatting methods like Pulsar) is detrimental. It causes unstable optimization and significant PSNR drops (e.g., 11dB in Truck scene) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Popping Artifacts:** Large Gaussians created during optimization can cause popping artifacts, often due to trivial rejection via guard bands in the rasterizer and depth order switches. A more principled culling approach is needed [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Isotropic vs. Anisotropic:** Optimizing a single scalar radius (isotropic) instead of the full covariance matrix (anisotropic) significantly reduces alignment with surfaces and rendering quality [S1, unofficial].
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Angular Missing Regions:** In captures with missing angular regions (e.g., corners, inside-out), optimizing all SH bands initially causes incorrect zero-order (diffuse) color values. This is mitigated by the staged SH introduction [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Gradient Limiting:** Limiting gradient computation to only the front-most $N$ points (e.g., $N=10$) causes unstable optimization and severe quality degradation (11dB PSNR drop in the Truck scene) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Popping Artifacts:** Large Gaussians created during optimization can cause popping artifacts, partly due to trivial rejection via guard bands in the rasterizer and depth order switches [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Training Time:**
-    *   Synthetic NeRF scenes: 30,000 iterations take approximately 35–45 minutes on a single NVIDIA A6000 GPU [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Mip-NeRF360 scenes: 48 hours on a single GPU (equivalent to 12 hours on a 4-GPU A100 node) [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Tanks & Temples scenes: ~7 minutes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Rendering Speed:** Trained synthetic scenes render at 180–300 FPS on an A6000 GPU [S1, unofficial]. Mip-NeRF360 renders at 10s/frame [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Model Size:**
-    *   Synthetic scenes: 200,000–500,000 Gaussians after 30,000 iterations [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Compactness: Uses ~1/4 the point count of Zhang et al. [2022] for comparable PSNR, resulting in 3.8 MB vs 9 MB model size [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:**
-    *   Fast convergence compared to NeRF-based methods (InstantNGP, Plenoxels) [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Anisotropic covariance allows better surface alignment [S1, unofficial].
-    *   SH bands effectively model view-dependent effects [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:**
-    *   Popping artifacts in regions with view-dependent appearance [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Elongated artifacts or "splotchy" Gaussians in poorly observed regions [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Quality degrades significantly if gradient computation is limited to front-most points [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Training Time:** For synthetic scenes, convergence to high quality occurs within 7,000 iterations (~8 minutes) [S1, unofficial]. For real-world scenes like Mip-NeRF360, training takes 35–45 minutes on a single A6000 GPU (equivalent to 12 hours on a 4-GPU A100 node for Mip-NeRF360, which takes 48 hours single-GPU equivalent) [S1, unofficial]. Tanks & Temples scenes take ~7 minutes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Rendering Speed:** Trained synthetic scenes render at 180–300 FPS [S1, unofficial]. Mip-NeRF360 renders at 10s/frame [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Model Size:** For synthetic scenes, the final model size after 30,000 iterations is 200,000–500,000 Gaussians [S1, unofficial]. Compared to Zhang et al. [2022], 3DGS uses ~1/4 the point count, resulting in a 3.8 MB model size vs 9 MB [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Quality:** Achieves comparable quality to InstantNGP/Plenoxels in 5–10 minutes, surpassing them with additional time. Matches or slightly exceeds Mip-NeRF360 quality after full convergence [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Anisotropic covariance significantly improves surface alignment and rendering quality compared to isotropic models [S1, unofficial]. Cloning small Gaussians aids convergence for thin structures; splitting big Gaussians aids background reconstruction [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Artifacts appear in poorly observed regions. Elongated or "splotchy" Gaussians may form. Popping artifacts can occur in regions with view-dependent appearance [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting optimization was introduced in the paper "3D Gaussian Splatting for Real-Time Radiance Field Rendering" by Kerbl et al. [2023]. The method builds on differentiable rasterization techniques extended from Kopanas et al. [2021] and uses NVIDIA CUB sorting routines for Radix sort [Merrill and Grimshaw 2010]. It was evaluated on 13 real scenes (Mip-NeRF360, Tanks&Temples, Hedman et al.) and synthetic Blender dataset scenes, using an A6000 GPU for most results [S1, unofficial].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. [2023] as a real-time radiance field rendering method [S1, unofficial]. The rasterization kernels are extended versions of methods by Kopanas et al. [2021], and the sorting uses routines from Merrill and Grimshaw [2010] [S1, unofficial]. The method was evaluated on 13 real scenes (Mip-NeRF360, Tanks&Temples, Hedman et al.) and the synthetic Blender dataset [S1, unofficial]. Hardware used was primarily A6000 GPU, except for Mip-NeRF360 which was trained on a 4-GPU A100 node [S1, unofficial].
 
